    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's a summary of the change implementing owner soft-delete.\n\n## What changed\n\n**Model** \u2014 `src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`\n- Added a persisted `deleted` boolean column (`@Column(name = \"deleted\")`) with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted(...)`. A primitive `boolean` defaults to `false`, so a newly created owner is not deleted.\n\n**API schema** \u2014 `src/main/resources/openapi.yml`\n- Added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it automatically. `GET /api/owners/{id}` now returns `deleted` (false for a fresh owner, true after deletion).\n\n**Controller** \u2014 `src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`\n- `deleteOwner(...)` now soft-deletes: it sets `deleted = true` and saves the owner (returning 204, a 2xx) instead of removing the row, so the record is retained and `GET` still returns it.\n- The create endpoint's checks now ignore deleted owners: `rejectHouseholdDuplicate` (the blocking duplicate check) and `findPossibleDuplicate` (the soft-match/identity check) both filter out `existing.isDeleted()`. A normally-blocking duplicate is therefore allowed when the only matching owner has been soft-deleted.\n\n**Schema/seed SQL** \u2014 `db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`\n- Added `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL` to the `owners` table. The tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so this schema is authoritative.\n- `db/hsqldb/data.sql`: the seed owner rows use positional `INSERT ... VALUES`, so I appended `FALSE` to each to match the new column count.\n\n## Verification\n`./mvnw test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, both new acceptance tests green:\n- `coreSoftDeleteFlagsAndRetains` \u2014 create shows `deleted=false`; after DELETE, GET returns 200 with `deleted=true`.\n- `coreDeletedOwnerIgnoredByDuplicateCheck` \u2014 re-creating an owner whose only match was soft-deleted is allowed (2xx).\n\nOne note: the `mysql`/`postgres` SQL was updated for schema consistency but isn't exercised by the test suite (postgres uses column-list inserts; the mysql seed already used a short positional insert list independent of this change).",
